1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,126
Two systems. Both made 30% in a year.

2
00:00:09,577 --> 00:00:13,208
The first account dipped only 8% along the way.

3
00:00:13,658 --> 00:00:16,584
The second fell 50%.

4
00:00:17,034 --> 00:00:23,147
On paper they are equal. In reality, most people would quit the second one halfway.

5
00:00:23,597 --> 00:00:26,444
That fall along the way is the drawdown.

6
00:00:27,444 --> 00:00:32,354
Hello, this is episode three of the SPIDER Quant Trading series.

7
00:00:32,805 --> 00:00:39,336
Last time: win rate and payoff, and why you must look at the biggest loss.

8
00:00:39,786 --> 00:00:47,257
Today we measure that pain: what drawdown is, how to count it, and why it matters most.

9
00:00:48,257 --> 00:00:52,541
The highest point the account reached is called the peak.

10
00:00:52,991 --> 00:00:56,543
How far it falls from that peak is the drawdown.

11
00:00:56,993 --> 00:01:02,140
From 100,000 to 120,000, then down to 90,000.

12
00:01:02,590 --> 00:01:08,545
A 30,000 fall, 25% of the peak. That is the drawdown.

13
00:01:09,545 --> 00:01:14,613
The biggest fall in the whole history is the max drawdown.

14
00:01:15,062 --> 00:01:19,818
Look at this number first in any backtest report. Profit comes later.

15
00:01:20,268 --> 00:01:27,112
Profit shows what you might get. Drawdown shows what you must endure on the way.

16
00:01:28,112 --> 00:01:33,832
Now the most dangerous part. Falling is easy, coming back is hard.

17
00:01:34,282 --> 00:01:38,880
Fall 10%, and you need 11% to recover.

18
00:01:39,330 --> 00:01:42,883
Fall 25%, and you need 33%.

19
00:01:43,333 --> 00:01:46,884
Fall 50%, and you need a full 100%.

20
00:01:47,335 --> 00:01:53,213
And fall 80%, you need 400%. Almost impossible.

21
00:01:54,213 --> 00:01:58,339
Why? Because after the fall, there is less money left.

22
00:01:58,789 --> 00:02:05,634
100 halves to 50. Now you must make 50 on 50, which is 100%.

23
00:02:06,083 --> 00:02:10,603
So the quant's first rule: do not allow a big drawdown.

24
00:02:11,603 --> 00:02:14,999
Drawdown is not just depth, it is also length.

25
00:02:15,448 --> 00:02:20,281
How long did it take to get back to a new peak?

26
00:02:20,732 --> 00:02:26,609
Many systems take months to reach a new peak. That is when patience breaks.

27
00:02:27,609 --> 00:02:30,117
So how do you compare two systems?

28
00:02:30,567 --> 00:02:34,433
An easy way: return divided by max drawdown.

29
00:02:34,882 --> 00:02:39,403
First system: 30 over 8, about 3.75.

30
00:02:39,852 --> 00:02:44,841
Second: 30 over 50, only 0.6.

31
00:02:45,292 --> 00:02:49,733
The bigger this number, the less pain for the same profit.

32
00:02:50,733 --> 00:02:56,297
A bitter truth: live drawdown is often bigger than the backtest.

33
00:02:56,747 --> 00:03:03,173
Spread, slippage and changing markets. A backtest does not capture all of it.

34
00:03:03,623 --> 00:03:10,388
So many traders assume the real drawdown can be 1.5 to 2 times the backtest.

35
00:03:11,388 --> 00:03:15,986
The biggest tool to control drawdown is position size.

36
00:03:16,436 --> 00:03:23,516
Risk 10% per trade, and five losses in a row wipe out about 40% of the account.

37
00:03:23,966 --> 00:03:29,216
Risk 1%, and the same five losses cost about 5%.

38
00:03:29,666 --> 00:03:36,352
Same system, same edge. Only size changed, and the pain is about eight times smaller.

39
00:03:37,352 --> 00:03:42,343
Last point. Before starting a system, set your limit.

40
00:03:42,793 --> 00:03:46,267
How much drawdown can I take without quitting the system?

41
00:03:46,716 --> 00:03:52,516
And at what point will I stop and re-check? Write it down first, not in the middle of the fall.

42
00:03:53,516 --> 00:03:55,084
So, today's summary.

43
00:03:55,534 --> 00:04:01,489
Drawdown is the fall from the peak. The biggest one is the max drawdown.

44
00:04:01,940 --> 00:04:06,380
Fall 50%, and you need 100% to recover.

45
00:04:06,829 --> 00:04:13,257
Compare systems by return over drawdown, and control drawdown with position size.

46
00:04:14,257 --> 00:04:19,011
Next episode: position sizing. How much to risk on each trade?

47
00:04:19,461 --> 00:04:24,372
If this was useful, subscribe and save the playlist.

48
00:04:24,822 --> 00:04:30,934
